msgid "Bruco: Bilinear Coupling and AM/FM Demodulation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/case-studies/case_bruco_advanced.ipynb:10004
#: ed2374b16c8149be90ecd7d7088045cd
msgid ""
"Standard Bruco scans only detect **linear** coherence between DARM and "
"auxiliary channels. Real interferometer noise often involves **nonlinear** "
"coupling mechanisms that a linear scan misses entirely. This tutorial covers"
" two common advanced patterns found in O4 commissioning data and distilled "
"from the gwexpy usage catalog:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/case-studies/case_bruco_advanced.ipynb:10009
#: bdf02daebd564c3fac73361cd345858f
msgid ""
"**Bilinear coupling detection** — build a synthetic witness `fast × slow` "
"and run Bruco on the extended channel set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/case-studies/case_bruco_advanced.ipynb:10011
#: 998f6c26c09e47acb6dddf889b40de1e
msgid ""
"**Hilbert AM/FM demodulation** — extract the instantaneous amplitude / "
"frequency of a spectral line and correlate these slowly-varying envelopes "
"with environmental sensors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/case-studies/case_bruco_advanced.ipynb:10014
#: 59be0d539b6b450ab40d62c24a1f2dac
msgid ""
"The physical question in both cases is the same: which slow environmental or"
" control variable is modulating a higher-frequency feature seen in DARM, and"
" how should we encode that hypothesis so Bruco can test it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/case-studies/case_bruco_advanced.ipynb:10016
#: 16016ecdaaa14bad888fb7ebe27fccc2
msgid "**Prerequisites**:"
msgstr "**前提条件**:"

